//- [CP4 v9] FAQ cluster A (after the Trust-DrySafe experience section).
//- Copy verbatim from SS88's drysafe_landing_faq_v3 (sign-off draft).
+faqClusterOpen
	+faqItem('What should I do before you arrive?', 'a0')
		p Very little, and the less you do the better.
		p Please don't scrub, spray or wipe the affected area before we get there. Disturbing growth makes it harder for us to see what we're dealing with and can spread the problem into rooms that were fine.
		p What does help:
		ul
			li #[strong Close the room off] if you reasonably can, and keep it that way until we arrive.
			li #[strong Stop any active water.] If there's a live leak, get a plumber to it first — that's a separate trade to ours and it needs to happen before or alongside our works.
			li #[strong Clear access.] Move small furniture, valuables and anything breakable out of the area if it's safe to do so. Leave the heavy items to us.
			li #[strong Take photos] of the area as it is now, and note roughly when you first saw it. Useful for your own records.
			li #[strong Have the story ready.] When you first noticed it, whether there'd been a leak, storm or flood beforehand, and whether anything has been done to it already.
			li #[strong Tell us about the household.] Pets, small children, anyone in the home with a health sensitivity, and any access constraints — parking, stairs, lift bookings, strata rules or building induction requirements.
	+faqItem('Do I need to leave my home during the works?', 'a1')
		p In most cases, no.
		p For a single localised area we set up containment around the work zone and work inside it, so the rest of the home stays usable. You'll lose access to that zone while we're in it, and there'll be equipment noise.
		p You're more likely to need to be out where the works are larger — multiple rooms, materials coming out, or the affected area covers the only bathroom or kitchen. If that applies to your job, we'll tell you before we start, not on the day, and it'll be written into the scope along with how long.
		p One thing we won't do is make a health call for you. If someone in the household has a medical condition and you want to know whether they should be in the property while works are underway, that's a question for their doctor. We're not qualified to answer it and we won't pretend otherwise.
